  "content": {
    "id": "p38-cc-7",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Bob and Mary are trying to differentiate $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{(2x+1)^3}$. Bob suggests that since the function is a fraction, they should use the quotient rule. Mary disagrees and says that only the chain rule is needed.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p38-cc-7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Differentiate $f(x)$ using Mary’s suggestion.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=-\\dfrac{12}{(2x+1)^4}$.",
          "worked": "$f(x)=2(2x+1)^{-3}$. \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=2(-3)(2x+1)^{-4}(2)\\\\f'(x)&=-\\dfrac{12}{(2x+1)^4}\\end{aligned}$$\n\n",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-cc-7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Differentiate $f(x)$ using Bob’s suggestion. Did it work?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=-\\dfrac{12}{(2x+1)^4}$. Yes, the quotient rule works, although it is impractical here.",
          "worked": "With $u=2$ and $v=(2x+1)^3$, $u'=0$ and $v'=6(2x+1)^2$. \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\dfrac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\dfrac{0-12(2x+1)^2}{(2x+1)^6}\\\\f'(x)&=-\\dfrac{12}{(2x+1)^4}\\end{aligned}$$\n\n Yes. The chain rule gives the same result more directly.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
